{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-dreaming/1488951-ctd-and-hunter-the-reckoning","id":1488951,"title":"CtD and Hunter the Reckoning","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Changeling: The Dreaming"],"posts":[{"date":"2022-08-03T01:39:44-07:00","id":1488951,"title":"CtD and Hunter the Reckoning","content":"Hello.<br /> <br /> Does anyone here have any experience with HtR and CtD interacting? Are there any official rule guides and such?<br /> <br /> I recently started playing in a hunter game, and my running character concept is a ex intelligence agent who's hunting sidhe and changeling nobility. The general gist is that on the outside he looks schizophrenic, rambling about elves and conspiracies, but if you dig, everything he is saying is true.<br /> <br /> How do hunter abilities such as second sight and judgement level 1: Discern, interact with changelings and the dreaming? Are hunter banal? What about memory loss which normal humans experience after seeing the dreaming? Does that affect the imbued?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25428-ebtard","name":"Ebtard","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"15"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-08-03T10:22:56-07:00","id":1488986,"title":"","content":"If you're just using the Imbued, I'd advise to stick to the HtR books. The two ST focused supplements have more advanced rules for the major supernaturals in an HtR context that don't diverge from the core rules in a fashion that would make Edges hard to figure out. Unless you are the ST, or your ST is looking for advice on how to merge the two game through your post here, you really don't need to delve into CtD (and you might end up making false conclusions as HtR advocates for not sticking to the other corebooks so players can't gain an advantage by reading those).<br /> <br /> There's no official crossover book for HtR and the other games (or really any of the old WoD game and each other) that addresses these topics in a thorough fashion. This is moreso true for Changeling because there, so far, hasn't been a time when CtD and HtR have been in publication at the same time (the original CtD was cancelled as a game line before HtR 1e was published, Changeling got a 20th book and HtR didn't, and CtD hasn't gotten a 5th edition book yet).<br /> <br /> So... &quot;how do these things interact?&quot; is mostly going to be how the ST decides they should. I would advise to err on the side of how Hunter intends for powers to work, because the Imbued are at a huge disadvantage to the full might of monsters made with the PC material from the other games. The broad immunities that Second Sight provides shouldn't be decreased or the Imbued don't stand a chance against even a starting level fae with the right Arts.<br /> <br /> Banality is a difficult discussion because it leads to a much larger thematic conflict between trying to run the two games together. The Imbued sit at a very weird place for changelings because the actions of the Imbued frequently make the coming Winter of dreams worse, but individually are also deeply immersed in the supernatural and know how frighteningly real the truth splendor of reality is. Reckoning wants the Imbued to be &quot;normal.&quot; They're supposed to be nearly impossible for monsters to identify as being special (even if that's just special compared to other overt monster hunters).<br /> <br /> So... on one hand there's a strong case that as they destroy fae and prodigal beings the Imbued should be higher on the banality scale based on their actions, but on the other there's a strong case that each individual Imbued (and esp. high level ones) as so immersed in how crazy their wold is that giving them high banality doesn't make a lick of sense.<br /> <br /> I tend to run the Imbued in crossover the few times I've done so as having low-ish Banality (5-6 for most, 3-4 for high Virtues, etc.). This makes them fairly weak to changelings while Second Sight is off, but when Second Sight is on, it's protections replace having high Banality to resist Arts like most Autumn servants have.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-08-03T13:30:55-07:00","id":1489007,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-dreaming/1488951-ctd-and-hunter-the-reckoning?p=1488986#post1488986\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Banality is a difficult discussion because it leads to a much larger thematic conflict between trying to run the two games together. The Imbued sit at a very weird place for changelings because the actions of the Imbued frequently make the coming Winter of dreams worse, but individually are also deeply immersed in the supernatural and know how frighteningly real the truth splendor of reality is. Reckoning wants the Imbued to be &quot;normal.&quot; They're supposed to be nearly impossible for monsters to identify as being special (even if that's just special compared to other overt monster hunters).<br /> <br /> So... on one hand there's a strong case that as they destroy fae and prodigal beings the Imbued should be higher on the banality scale based on their actions, but on the other there's a strong case that each individual Imbued (and esp. high level ones) as so immersed in how crazy their wold is that giving them high banality doesn't make a lick of sense.<br /> <br /> I tend to run the Imbued in crossover the few times I've done so as having low-ish Banality (5-6 for most, 3-4 for high Virtues, etc.). This makes them fairly weak to changelings while Second Sight is off, but when Second Sight is on, it's protections replace having high Banality to resist Arts like most Autumn servants have.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> I fully expect my character to be high banality, basically a autum person gone self aware, in a sense.<br /> <br /> His story is pretty whacky, but in a good hunter way i think. He was a KGB agent during the fall of the USSR, who realized that his superior and a good deal of the high up beaurocracy where sidhe elves. Naturally, he killed his skinwalker boss and fled off grid.<br /> <br /> His knowledge of this hidden world has radicalized him in his beliefs. He is convinced that the only reason communism failed was because it was undermined be elves and other extradimensional enteties. The only way for true socialism to triumf will be to eradicate the very dream of capitalism and heirarchy that the sidhe represent. With the beloved symbol of the proletariot and industry, iron.<br /> <br /> Because from a socialist perspective, the sidhe and the dreamings endorcement of them is completly unacceptable. And the only solution to a kind of being where their heirarchy and class are literally written into the and endorced by the universe itself, is extreme violence.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25428-ebtard","name":"Ebtard","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"15"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-08-03T15:52:17-07:00","id":1489020,"title":"","content":"The problem is that none of that really follows the specific idea of what Banality is in CtD. If simply opposing Sidhe power hierarchies was Banal, the Common cause would have imploded rather than there being a war for power when the Sidhe returned. Plenty of changelings (while they might not be Soviets in thinking) don't want the Sidhe way of rule too.<br /> <br /> This is also hard to judge because when rigid ideology turns into Banality isn't exactly clear. C20 has an Akashic mage with lowish Banality despite their regimented and detached lifestyle dictated by their method of magic. There are plenty of supernaturals in the 4-6 Banality range that are adamant socialists (even if more modern in their formulation of politics).<br /> <br /> Your character rejecting the real corruption of Soviet bureaucracy for a more ideal socialism devoid of the temptations of wealth and inequity is somewhat neutral towards how the books describe banality.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}